# COMPONENTS

Identify every architectural component mentioned or clearly described (service, application,
microservice, batch job, data pipeline, database, queue, topic, event processor, API, external
system, data platform, storage system, or other architectural building block).

**Queue/Topic vs. data contract — do not double-list.** A named event/message/data flow you're
identifying as a data contract below is not *also* a component just because it moves over a
queue or topic. "Topic"/"Queue" is a component only when the underlying broker/infrastructure
itself is being discussed as a building block (e.g. "we're sharding the order-events topic
across three partitions") — not merely that a contract happens to be delivered over one.

For every component, determine its status:

* **`new`** — the transcript establishes it's being introduced and it has no match in
  `KNOWN_ARCHITECTURE`.
* **`modified`** — an existing component changes its responsibilities, behavior, interfaces,
  dependencies, data flows, data contracts, or other architectural characteristics.
* **`removed`** — the transcript establishes it will be removed.
* **`unchanged`** — it exists and is part of the architecture involved in the change, but the
  transcript never establishes a change to it. Never use this merely because the transcript
  doesn't mention a change — its role must be established, not just absent from discussion.
* **`unknown`** — impossible to determine which of the above applies. Never guess.

Never infer status from a component's name alone ("Event Processor" establishes nothing by
itself). "We are replacing the existing Event Processor" establishes `removed` (and probably a
`new` replacement); "We will add customer enrichment to the Event Processor" (already in
`KNOWN_ARCHITECTURE`) establishes `modified`; "We are introducing a new Event Processor"
establishes `new`. A transcript/known-architecture contradiction that can't be resolved is
`unknown`.

**No-change safeguard.** If, across the whole transcript, not a single component lands on
`new`/`modified`/`removed` — everything is `unchanged` or `unknown` — classify each one exactly
per the rules above anyway, without forcing a status just to avoid an all-`unchanged` result.
This is a legitimate outcome (a purely descriptive discussion of an existing system) — call 2
checks for it directly in your output and drafts the appropriate question, so nothing extra is
needed from you here beyond classifying honestly.

---

# DATA CONTRACTS

Identify every data contract explicitly mentioned or clearly established — an event, message,
API request/response, file, dataset, table, stream, data product, or other exchanged data
structure. Identification only: name, producer, consumer, action. Never ask about or record
schema, quality, SLA, or full ODCS metadata — a later stage handles that once these are
identified.

**Connections imply contracts — unconditional.** A component-to-component connection ALWAYS
gets a data contract candidate unless BOTH components are confirmed `unchanged`. This needs no
contract-specific vocabulary ("event"/"message"/"stream") and no named payload to qualify — "the
frontend calls the backend over REST", "the backend reads from the database" are each, by
themselves, exactly as sufficient as a transcript that explicitly says "event" or "schema." The
transport being generic is a description of transport, never a reason to skip identifying the
contract. This includes a connection where a component's status is still `unknown` — unresolved
is not "safe to skip"; only a status CONFIRMED `unchanged` is grounds to skip. List one
candidate per connection *direction* — a call and its response are two separate entries, never
one bidirectional one. Use `"unknown"` for `name` when the transcript never names the contract
itself (only the connection) — never invent a plausible-sounding name.

The only exception: purely internal interactions never qualify, regardless of component status —
an internal method call, a private implementation detail, UI component props, an in-process
transformation, or any communication with no independent data ownership. "It's just REST/HTTP"
or "it's just a database call" is NOT this exception — a connection between two distinct
components (or a component and its database) always crosses a boundary, no matter how ordinary
the transport.

**Technical interface vs. data contract — do not conflate.** A technical interface (REST,
GraphQL, gRPC, a Kafka topic, a database interface) is the transport; a data contract is the
agreed structure/semantics/ownership/quality/compatibility of what flows across it. They are
related but not the same artifact. This distinction matters for how call 2 will phrase
questions about each — it is not a reason to skip identifying a contract here.

**Action** — determine from the transcript and known architecture:

* `new` — a new contract.
* `forward-update` — existing consumers keep working unmodified (an additive optional field, a
  new enum value alongside old ones, a loosened constraint).
* `break-change` — an existing consumer would need to change (a required field removed/renamed,
  a type narrowed, a previously optional field made required, a removed enum value a consumer
  might still send/expect). Judge by consumer compatibility, never by how large the change
  sounds.
* `unchanged` — an existing contract with no established change.
* `deprecated` — replaced by a new version.
* `removed` — no longer used.
* `unknown` — cannot be determined, including whether a schema change is compatible or
  breaking.

**Producer/consumer** — never infer from component names alone; only from what the transcript
actually states. Use `"unknown"` for whichever side isn't stated. A contract with an unresolved
producer or consumer still belongs in the output (marked `"unknown"`) — never omitted for that
reason.

---

# GROUNDING — DO NOT INVENT

Never invent a component, a component's status, a data contract, a producer, a consumer, a
technology, a protocol, or an action. Every `mentioned_components`/`mentioned_data_contracts`
entry must be grounded in the transcript (or, for status/action, in the transcript together with
`KNOWN_ARCHITECTURE`) — a structural check verifies every component name appears in the
transcript verbatim. When something can't be determined, mark it `"unknown"`; do not guess a
cleaner-looking answer.

---

# OUTPUT FORMAT

Return exactly one valid JSON object. No Markdown, no code fences, no text before or after it.

```json
{
  "mentioned_components": [
    {"name": "...", "status": "new|modified|removed|unchanged|unknown"}
  ],
  "mentioned_data_contracts": [
    {"name": "...", "producer": "...", "consumer": "...", "action": "new|forward-update|break-change|unchanged|deprecated|removed|unknown"}
  ]
}
```

Use `"unknown"` (never omission) for a name/producer/consumer/action/status that cannot be
determined. Every component actually named or clearly described in the transcript gets one
entry; every data contract identified above (explicit or connection-implied) gets one entry.

